35,2,1,talk,Asuka,,The data confirms the bottleneck. Four out of eleven mornings required shifting bikes. The rain exposure is also a critical factor for bike condition.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,The ledger shows no capital funds. Any new system requires a household levy. The treasurer already spends three hours monthly on cash.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,George,,The minutes confirm my fear. The same flats held the covered spots for five years with no review. The sticker register shows riders prefer paying over equal claims.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,"Nine complaints, all ignored. The block behind us lost two members to a fee. We are one bad decision away from the same silence.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Mio,,"The frame trial was brutal. Hoisting a bike took twenty seconds and both hands. Plus, the spring needs greasing twice a year. That is a lot of physical work.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Ryuji, a levy is risky. If we add cost to the existing friction, people will just stop cycling. We need a solution that works without new money.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, I agree. Adding a levy to existing friction is dangerous. We must find a solution that does not require new cash from households.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,George,,"@Shunichi, nine ignored complaints explain the resentment. The minutes prove the spots were never reviewed, so the current layout is pure inertia, not need.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@George, inertia is a slow poison. The block behind us proves that fees kill participation. We need a system that costs nothing but still keeps people talking.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Mio,,"@George, five years of no review is terrifying. It means the current setup is just luck, not design. I need a system that doesn't rely on one person remembering to fix it.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, I agree. The frame trial proves manual maintenance is unsustainable. We need a layout that minimizes shifting, not a gadget that breaks.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Mio, I agree. If the frame fails, the workload collapses. We need a layout that is self-sustaining without constant manual intervention.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,George,,"@Mio, I agree. Inertia is not a plan. We need a review cycle that is automatic, not dependent on someone remembering to act.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Mio, exactly. If the system dies, the silence returns. We need a layout that is self-sustaining, so nobody has to be the hero.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Mio,,"@Shunichi, I agree. The greasing alone is a trap. I want a layout where the work is so small, nobody feels the burden of doing it.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
