60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,Option B eliminates the shifting bottleneck. No fixed spots means no one is blocked. It also solves the rain issue if we place racks under the eaves.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option A is a trap. The ledger shows no capital funds, and the treasurer is already stretched. I cannot support a system that requires new levies.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,"Option C is a return to the same inertia. The minutes prove that fixed spots, even by lot, just harden into habit.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,Option A is the block behind us. Fees kill participation. I refuse to turn our lobby into a collection point for resentment.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Mio,,Option B is the only one that doesn't create a new maintenance trap. No fixed spots means no one is stuck with the greasing duty.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Ryuji, I agree. Option B requires no new cash, just a one-time rack purchase. It solves the bottleneck without adding financial pressure.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, I agree. Option B avoids the levy trap. The one-time cost is manageable, unlike the recurring burden of A.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,"@Shunichi, I agree. Option B removes the ownership that breeds resentment. It is the only way to break the five-year inertia we found.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@George, exactly. If no one owns a spot, no one has a reason to stop talking to their neighbor. Option B is the only one that keeps the peace.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Mio,,"@George, I agree. If no one owns a spot, the greasing duty doesn't attach to a specific person. It just becomes a shared, minor task.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, precisely. Shared racks mean the maintenance is trivial and distributed. No single person bears the burden of a specific, neglected spot.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Mio, I agree. Distributed maintenance is the only way to prevent the workload from collapsing on one person.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,"@Mio, I agree. Shared racks mean the maintenance is trivial and distributed. No single person bears the burden of a specific, neglected spot.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Mio, exactly. If the work is shared, nobody feels the weight of it. That is how we keep the peace without a hero.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Mio,,"@Shunichi, I agree. If the work is shared, nobody feels the weight of it. That is how we keep the peace without a hero.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
